&= \boxed{\dfrac{3}{2}}
\end{align*}
where we calculate  $b_0$  using the given recurrence. Note that the sum on the second line telescopes/converges due to the boundedness of  $b$ .  $\square$

We have that   $$ b_n = a_2 a_3 \dots a_n - 2 a_3 a_4 \dots a_n - 2 a_4 \dots a_n - \dots - 2 $$   Then the partial sum evaluates to: 
\begin{align*}
S &=\frac{1}{a_1} + \frac{1}{a_1 a_2} + \dots + \frac{1}{a_1 a_2 \dots a_n} 
&= \frac{a_2 a_3 \dots a_n + a_3 \dots a_n + \dots + 1}{a_1 a_2 \dots a_n}  
&= \frac{a_1 \dots a_n + \frac{a_1 \dots a_n - b_n}{2}}{a_1 \dots a_n}  
&= \frac32 - \frac{b_n}{2(a_2 a_3 \dots a_n)} 
\end{align*}
But clearly when we take  $n \Rightarrow \infty$  we have  $b_n$  bounded and  $a_2 \dots a_n$  unbounded  because it is equal to:   $$ \frac{b_2+2}{b_1} \cdot \frac{b_3+2}{b_2} \cdot \dots \cdot \frac{b_n+2}{b_{n-1}} = \frac{b_n+2}{b_1} \prod \frac{b_n+2}{b_n} $$   Thus our answer is  $\boxed{\frac32}$

Here's another way; as above  $c=-1$  and  $L=2/\pi$ .

Integrate by parts to get
\begin{align*}
\int_{0}^{\pi/2}x^r\sin{x}\,dx
&=
-x^r\cos{x}\bigg|_{0}^{\pi/2}+r\int_{0}^{\pi/2}x^{r-1}\cos{x}\,dx
&
=
r\int_0^{\pi/2}x^{r-1}\cos{x}\,dx.
\end{align*}
If  $c=-1$  and  $\epsilon>0$  is arbitrary, the expression in the problem statement becomes
\begin{align}
\frac{\int_0^{\pi/2}x^{r-1}\cos x\,dx}{\int_0^{\pi/2}x^r\cos{x}\,dx}
&=
\frac{2}{\pi}\frac{\int_0^{\pi/2}\left(\frac{x}{\pi/2}\right)^{r-1}\cos x\,dx}{\int_0^{\pi/2}\left(\frac{x}{\pi/2}\right)^r\cos{x}\,dx}
&=
\frac{2}{\pi}\frac{\int_0^{\pi/2-\epsilon}\left(\frac{x}{\pi/2}\right)^{r-1}\cos x\,dx+\int_{\pi/2-\epsilon}^{\pi/2}\left(\frac{x}{\pi/2}\right)^{r-1}\cos x\,dx}{\int_0^{\pi/2-\epsilon}\left(\frac{x}{\pi/2}\right)^r\cos{x}\,dx+\int_{\pi/2-\epsilon}^{\pi/2}\left(\frac{x}{\pi/2}\right)^r\cos{x}\,dx},
\nonumber
\end{align}
which, in the limit as  $r\to\infty$ , has the same behavior as
\begin{align*}
\frac{2}{\pi}
\frac{\int_{\pi/2-\epsilon}^{\pi/2}\left(\frac{x}{\pi/2}\right)^{r-1}\cos x\,dx}{\int_{\pi/2-\epsilon}^{\pi/2}\left(\frac{x}{\pi/2}\right)^r\cos x\,dx}
\leq
\frac{2}{\pi}\frac{\pi/2}{\pi/2-\epsilon}.
\end{align*}
Since the left side of (1) is clearly  $\geq\pi/2$ , letting  $\epsilon\to0$  gives the result. (Too bad I wasted all that time on the exam with A1...)

Evaluating the limit as  $r\to\infty$  of expressions like (1) is a not uncommon textbook exercise, see Rudin's Real & Complex Analysis, Exercise 18, Ch.3; I believe it's in Folland's analysis book too. There it's phrased: If  $f\in L^1(X,\mu)\cap L^{\infty}(X,\mu)$ ,  $\mu(X)<\infty$ , and  $\|f\|_{\infty}>0$ , then  $\lim_{p\to\infty}\|f\|_{p+1}^{p+1}/\|f\|_p^p=\|f\|_{\infty}$ . In the case of the Putnam problem,  $d\mu=\cos{x}\,dx$ ,  $X=[0,\pi/2]$ , and  $f(x)=x$ .

Here's a solution that only uses Taylor series. The answer is \(c = -1\) and \(L = 2/\pi\).
The series for \(\sin x\) and \(\cos x\) are
    \begin{align*}
        \sin x  &= \sum_{i=0}^\infty \frac{(-1)^i}{(2i+1)!} x^{2i+1} 
        \cos x  &= \sum_{i=0}^\infty \frac{(-1)^i}{(2i)!} x^{2i}.
    \end{align*}**Lemma 1.** \[ \lim_{r\to \infty} {r(\pi/2)^{-r-1} \int_0^{\pi/2} x^r \sin x\,dx} = 1\]
<details><summary>Proof</summary>\begin{align*}
        &\lim_{r\to \infty}
        {r(\pi/2)^{-r-1} \int_0^{\pi/2} x^r \sin x\,dx} 
        =& \lim_{r\to \infty}
        {\int_0^{\pi/2} \sum_{i=0}^\infty r(\pi/2)^{-r-1}\frac{(-1)^i}{(2i+1)!} x^{2i+1+r}\,dx} 
        =& \lim_{r\to \infty}
        {\sum_{i=0}^\infty \int_0^{\pi/2} r(\pi/2)^{-r-1}\frac{(-1)^i}{(2i+1)!} x^{2i+1+r}\,dx} 
        =& \lim_{r\to \infty}
        {\sum_{i=0}^\infty r(\pi/2)^{-r-1}\frac{(-1)^i}{(2i+2+r)(2i+1)!} \left(\pi/2\right)^{2i+2+r}} 
        =& \lim_{r\to \infty}
        {\sum_{i=0}^\infty \frac{r}{2i+2+r}\frac{(-1)^i}{(2i+1)!} \left(\pi/2\right)^{2i+1}} 
        =& \sum_{i=0}^\infty \lim_{r\to \infty}
        {\frac{r}{2i+2+r}\frac{(-1)^i}{(2i+1)!} \left(\pi/2\right)^{2i+1}} 
        =& \sum_{i=0}^\infty
        {\frac{(-1)^i}{(2i+1)!} \left(\pi/2\right)^{2i+1}} 
        =& \sum_{i=0}^\infty
        {\frac{(-1)^i}{(2i+1)!} \left(\pi/2\right)^{2i+1}} 
        =& \sin\left(\pi/2\right) = 1
    \end{align*}</details>**Lemma 2.** \[\lim_{r\to \infty}
        r^2(\pi/2)^{-r-1} \int_0^{\pi/2} x^r \cos x\,dx = \pi/2\]
<details><summary>Proof</summary>\begin{align*}
        &\lim_{r\to \infty}
        r^2(\pi/2)^{-r-1} \int_0^{\pi/2} x^r \cos x\,dx
        =& \lim_{r\to \infty}
        \int_0^{\pi/2} \sum_{i=0}^\infty r^2(\pi/2)^{-r-1} \frac{(-1)^i}{(2i)!} x^{2i}\,dx 
        =& \lim_{r\to \infty}
        \sum_{i=0}^\infty \int_0^{\pi/2} r^2(\pi/2)^{-r-1} \frac{(-1)^i}{(2i)!} x^{2i}\,dx 
        =& \lim_{r\to \infty}
        \sum_{i=0}^\infty \frac{r^2}{2i+1+r}\frac{(-1)^i}{(2i)!} (\pi/2)^{2i} 
        =& \lim_{r\to \infty} \left(
            \frac{r^2}{r+1} + \sum_{i=1}^\infty \frac{r^2}{2i+1+r}\frac{(-1)^i}{(2i)!} (\pi/2)^{2i}
        \right) 
        =& \lim_{r\to \infty} \left(
            \frac{r^2}{r+1} + \sum_{i=1}^\infty \frac{r^2}{(2i+1+r)(2i)}\frac{(-1)^i}{(2i-1)!} (\pi/2)^{2i}
        \right) 
        =& \lim_{r\to \infty} \left(
            \frac{r^2}{r+1} + \sum_{i=1}^\infty \frac{r}{r+1}\left(\frac{r}{2i} - \frac{r}{2i+1+r}\right)\frac{(-1)^i}{(2i-1)!} (\pi/2)^{2i}
        \right) 
        =& \lim_{r\to \infty} \left(
            \left(\sum_{i=0}^\infty \frac{r^2}{r+1}\frac{(-1)^i}{(2i)!} (\pi/2)^{2i}\right) - 
            \left(\sum_{i=1}^\infty \frac{r^2}{(r+1)(2i+1+r)}\frac{(-1)^i}{(2i-1)!} (\pi/2)^{2i}\right)
        \right) 
        =& \lim_{r\to \infty} \left(
            \frac{r^2}{r+1}\left(\sum_{i=0}^\infty \frac{(-1)^i}{(2i)!} (\pi/2)^{2i}\right) - 
            \left(\sum_{i=1}^\infty \frac{r^2}{(r+1)(2i+1+r)}\frac{(-1)^i}{(2i-1)!} (\pi/2)^{2i}\right)
        \right) 
        =& \lim_{r\to \infty} \left(
            \frac{r^2}{r+1}\cos(\pi/2) - 
            \left(\sum_{i=1}^\infty \frac{r^2}{(r+1)(2i+1+r)}\frac{(-1)^i}{(2i-1)!} (\pi/2)^{2i}\right) 
        \right) 
        =& \lim_{r\to \infty}
        \sum_{i=1}^\infty \frac{r^2}{(r+1)(2i+1+r)}\frac{(-1)^{i+1}}{(2i-1)!} (\pi/2)^{2i} 
        =& \sum_{i=1}^\infty \lim_{r\to \infty} \frac{r^2}{(r+1)(2i+1+r)}\frac{(-1)^{i+1}}{(2i-1)!} (\pi/2)^{2i} 
        =& \sum_{i=1}^\infty \frac{(-1)^{i+1}}{(2i-1)!} (\pi/2)^{2i}
        = \sum_{i=0}^\infty \frac{(-1)^{i}}{(2i+1)!} (\pi/2)^{2i+2} 
        =& (\pi/2) \sum_{i=0}^\infty \frac{(-1)^{i}}{(2i+1)!} (\pi/2)^{2i+1}
        = (\pi/2)\sin(\pi/2) = \pi/2.
    \end{align*}</details>
Finally, this implies that
    \begin{align*}
        \lim_{r\to \infty}
        \frac%
        {r^{-1} \int_0^{\pi/2} x^r \sin x\,dx}%
        {\int_0^{\pi/2} x^r \cos x\,dx}
        &=
        \lim_{r\to \infty}
        \frac%
        {r  (\pi/2)^{-r-1} \int_0^{\pi/2} x^r \sin x\,dx}%
        {r^2(\pi/2)^{-r-1} \int_0^{\pi/2} x^r \cos x\,dx}
        &=
        \frac%
        {\lim_{r\to \infty}r  (\pi/2)^{-r-1} \int_0^{\pi/2} x^r \sin x\,dx}%
        {\lim_{r\to \infty}r^2(\pi/2)^{-r-1} \int_0^{\pi/2} x^r \cos x\,dx}
        = \frac{2}{\pi}
    \end{align*}

