Here is a solution using the structure theorem and using generating functions. The key motivation is the cyclic scenario. 

We will just use  $G = \bigoplus_{j=1}^m \mathbb{Z} / n_j \mathbb{Z}$ . Let  $e = (0,\cdots,0)$  and  $g_j = (g_{j,1}, \cdots, g_{j,m})$ . Let   $$ P(x_1, \cdots, x_m) = \sum\limits_{j=1}^k \prod\limits_{l=1}^m x_l^{g_{j,l}} $$  We consider the behavior of  $|P(x_1,\cdots,x_m)|$  when  $x_j$  is a  $n_j$ th root of unity. It is  $k$  when  $x_1=x_2=\cdots=x_m=1$ , and for it to be  $k$ , by triangular inequality,   $$ \prod\limits_{l=1}^m x_l^{g_{j,l}}=1 $$  for all  $j=1,\cdots,k$ . We can then use the fact that  $g_j$  generate all elements of  $G$  to see that  $x_1 = \cdots = x_m$  is forced. For all integers  $y$ , define  $\omega_y = \exp(2\pi i/y)$ One can show that   $$ \mathbb{P}(g_1 + \cdots +g_t = (c_1,\cdots,c_m)) = k^{-t} \sum_{(t_1,\cdots,t_m)} \frac{1}{|G|} \sum_{0\le k_j\le n_j-1} \prod \omega_{n_j}^{k_j(t_j-c_j)} = \frac{1}{k^t|G|} \sum_{0\le k_j\le n_j-1} (\prod \omega_{n_j}^{-k_jc_j})\sum_{(t_1,\cdots,t_m)}  \prod_{j=1}^m (\omega_{n_j}^{k_j})^{t_j} $$  Where every way  $(t_1,\cdots,t_m) = a_1+\cdots+a_t$  ( $a_j$  picked from  $g_1,\cdots,g_k$ ) is counted once in  $\sum_{(t_1,\cdots,t_m)}$ . Then define  $t_j = a_{1,j}+\cdots+a_{t,j}$  in  $\mathbb{Z}/n_j\mathbb{Z}$ , letting  $\zeta_j = \omega_{n_j}^{k_j}$   $$ \sum_{a_p \in \{g_1,\cdots,g_k\}}  \prod_{j=1}^m (\zeta_j)^{a_{1,j}+\cdots+a_{t,j}} =\sum_{a_p \in \{g_1,\cdots,g_k\}}  \prod_{j=1}^m \prod_{u=1}^t (\zeta_j)^{a_{u,j}} = \sum_{a_p \in \{g_1,\cdots,g_k\}}  \prod_{u=1}^t \prod_{j=1}^m (\zeta_j)^{a_{u,j}} = P(\zeta_1,\cdots,\zeta_m)^t $$  Thus, \begin{align*}
&\sum_{(c_1,\cdots,c_m) \in G} (\mathbb{P}(g_1 + \cdots +g_t = (c_1,\cdots,c_m)) - 1/n)^2 
& =\sum_{(c_1,\cdots,c_m)\in G} \left(\frac{1}{|G|} \left(\sum_{0\le k_j\le n_j-1} (\prod \omega_{n_j}^{-k_jc_j}) (P(\omega_{n_1}^{k_1}, \cdots, \omega_{n_m}^{k_m})/k)^t \right) - 1/{|G|}\right)^2 
&=\sum_{(c_1,\cdots,c_m)\in G} \left(\frac{1}{|G|} \left(\sum_{\substack{0\le k_j\le n_j-1,  k_j \text{ not all zero}}} (\prod \omega_{n_j}^{-k_jc_j}) (P(\omega_{n_1}^{k_1}, \cdots, \omega_{n_m}^{k_m})/k)^t \right) \right)^2 
&= |G|^{-2} \sum_{(c_1,\cdots,c_m)\in G} \left(\sum_{\substack{0\le k_j\le n_j-1,  k_j \text{ not all zero}}} (\prod \omega_{n_j}^{-k_jc_j}) (P(\omega_{n_1}^{k_1}, \cdots, \omega_{n_m}^{k_m})/k)^t  \right)\left( \sum_{\substack{0\le l_j\le n_j-1,  l_j \text{ not all zero}}} (\prod \omega_{n_j}^{-l_jc_j}) (P(\omega_{n_1}^{l_1}, \cdots, \omega_{n_m}^{l_m})/k)^t \right)  
&=  |G|^{-2} \sum_{\substack{0\le k_j\le n_j-1,  k_j \text{ not all zero}}}\sum_{\substack{0\le l_j\le n_j-1,  l_j \text{ not all zero}}} P(\omega_{n_1}^{k_1}, \cdots, \omega_{n_m}^{k_m})/k)^t P(\omega_{n_1}^{l_1}, \cdots, \omega_{n_m}^{l_m})/k)^t  \sum_{(c_1,\cdots,c_m)\in G} \prod \omega_{n_j}^{-(k_j+l_j)c_j} 
\end{align*}

(Here  $(c_1,\cdots,c_m)\in G$  imply  $c_j \in \mathbb{Z}/n_j\mathbb{Z}$ . )

The key observation now is that if  $k_j+l_j$  is not  $0$  in  $\mathbb{Z}/n_j\mathbb{Z}$  for any  $j$ , then the contribution of  $(k_1,\cdots,k_m), (l_1,\cdots,l_m)$  is 0. Therefore the sum is merely  $$  |G|^{-2} \sum_{\substack{0\le k_j\le n_j-1,  k_j \text{ not all zero}}} |P(\omega_{n_1}^{k_1}, \cdots, \omega_{n_m}^{k_m})/k)^t|^2  (\sum_{(c_1,\cdots,c_m)\in G} 1) = |G|^{-1} \sum_{\substack{0\le k_j\le n_j-1,  k_j \text{ not all zero}}} |P(\omega_{n_1}^{k_1}, \cdots, \omega_{n_m}^{k_m})/k)^t|^2 $$  Let   $$ b = \frac 1k \max_{\substack{0\le k_j\le n_j-1,  k_j \text{ not all zero}}} |P(\omega_{n_1}^{k_1}, \cdots, \omega_{n_m}^{k_m})| $$  which works.

