Let  $P(a,b,c,d)$  be the assertion that  $(f(a)+f(b))(f(c)+f(d))=(a+b+c+d)$  $P\left(x,\frac 1x, x, \frac1x \right)\implies\left(f(x)+f\left(\frac1x\right)\right)^2=\left(x+\frac1x\right)^2$ Since  $f(x)+f\left(\frac1x\right)>0$ , it follows that:  $$ f(x)+f\left(\frac1x\right)=x+\frac1x $$   $P\left(x,x, \frac1x, \frac1x \right)\implies (2f(x))\left(2f\left(\frac1x\right)\right)=(2x)\left(\frac2x\right)$ or:   $$ f(x)f\left(\frac1x\right)=1 $$  With this we substitute  $f\left(\frac1x\right)=\frac1{f(x)}$  into  $f(x)+f\left(\frac1x\right)=x+\frac1x$ .  $$ f(x)+\frac1{f(x)}=x+\frac1x $$    $$ f(x)^2-\left(x+\frac1x\right)f(x)+1=0 $$  Using the Quadratic Formula:  $$ \begin{aligned}
f(x)&=\frac{x+\frac1x\pm\sqrt{\left(x+\frac1x\right)^2-4}}{2}
&=\frac{x+\frac1x\pm\sqrt{x^2-2+\frac1{x^2}}}{2}
&=\frac{x+\frac1x\pm\left(x-\frac1x\right)}{2}
&=x \text{ or } \frac1x
\end{aligned} $$  Now assume for a solution  $f$  there exists two distinct positive reals  $x,y$  such that  $f(x)=x$  and  $f(y)=\frac1y$ From the condition  $f(x)f\left(\frac1x\right)=1$  we have  $f\left(\frac1x\right)=\frac1x$  and  $f\left(\frac1y\right)=y$ Then:  $P\left(x,y,\frac1x,\frac1y\right)\implies \left(x+\frac1y\right)\left(\frac1x+y\right)=(x+y)\left(\frac1x+\frac1y\right)\iff(ab+1)^2=(a+b)^2$ Which is false.

Thus  $f(x)\equiv x$  and  $f(x)\equiv\frac1x$  are the only solutions.

