\begin{align*}
f(x) &= \cos x + \int_0^{2\pi}f(y)\sin(x-y)dy
&=\cos x + \int_0^{2\pi}f(y)(\sin x\cos y-\cos x\sin y)dy
&=\left(1-\int_0^{2\pi}f(y)\sin ydy\right)\cos x + \left(\int_0^{2\pi}f(y)\cos y dy\right)\sin x
&\equiv A \cos x + B\sin x
\end{align*}

From this we obtain:
\begin{align}
A & = 1-\int_0^{2\pi}f(y)\sin ydy = 1 - \int_0^{2\pi} (A\cos y + B\sin y)\sin y dy = 1-\pi B
B &=\int_0^{2\pi}(A\cos y + B\sin y)\cos y dy = \pi A
\end{align}

Solving (1) and (2) we get
\begin{align*}
A &=\frac{1}{1+\pi^2}
B &=\frac{\pi}{1+\pi^2}
\end{align*}

Therefore $f(x) = \frac{\cos x+\pi\sin x}{1+\pi^2}$

