<details><summary>Solution</summary>Let  $BE$  and  $AC$  intersect at a point  $Q$  and let  $O$  be the circumcenter of  $ABCDE$ . Let  $\angle CBQ=\alpha$  and let  $\angle CQB=\theta$ . This means  $\angle BCQ=180-\theta-\alpha$ . Since  $\angle BCQ$  and  $\angle BEA$  subtend the same arc  $AB$  we know that  $\angle BEA=180-\theta-\alpha$ . Since  $AC$  and  $DE$  are parallel we know that  $\angle DEQ=\theta$  because of corresponding angles. Since opposite angles of cyclic quadrilateral  $BCDE$  must add to  $180^{\circ}$  we know that  $\angle ACD=\alpha$ . This means that trapezoid  $ACDE$  is an isosceles trapezoid because the base angles are equal. Since this is an isosceles trapezoid we know that  $\overarc{AE}=\overarc{DC}$  as they are subtended by equal length segments. Now notice that  $\angle PQB=\frac{\overarc{AE}+\overarc{BC}}{2}=\frac{\overarc{CD}+\overarc{BC}}{2}=\frac{\overarc{DB}}{2}=\angle POB$ . We can see from here that we have a cyclic quadrilateral  $POQB$ . It follows that  $\angle OQP=\angle OBP = 90^{\circ}$ . Now we can conclude that  $Q$  is the midpoint of  $AC$  and that  $BE$  bisects  $AC$ .</details>

