Let  $O$  be the center of  $\omega$ . It suffices to show that  $OM \perp AC$ .**<span style="color:#f00">Claim.**</span>  $OMBP$  is cyclic.

*Proof.* We have   $$ \measuredangle BMP = \measuredangle EMC = \measuredangle MED = \frac 12 \overarc{ $AD$ }, $$   and trivially we also have   $$ \measuredangle BOP = \frac 12 \overarc{ $AD$ } $$   since  $OP$  bisects arc  $BD$  by symmetry. Then  $\measuredangle BMP = \measuredangle BOP$  implies  $BMOP$  cyclic.  $\blacksquare$ Then  $\measuredangle OMP = \measuredangle OBP = 90^{\circ}$  implies that  $M$  is the midpoint of  $AC$  as desired.  $\blacksquare$</details>

Let  $(ABCDE)$  be the unit circle. For points  $A, B, C, D, E, P$  let the complex numbers  $a, b, c, d, e, p$  represent them respectively. Notice that

\begin{align*}
    p&=\frac{2bd}{b+d} 
    c &= \frac{2bd-ab-ad}{b+d-2a} 
    e &= \frac{a(2bd-ab-ad)}{d(b+d-2a)}
\end{align*}

Now all we have to do is plug this into the complex intersection formula and compare this number with  $\frac{a+c}{2}$ .

We have
\begin{align*}
    be &= \frac{ab(2bd-ab-ad)}{d(b+d-2a)} 
    ac &= \frac{a(2bd-ab-ad)}{b+d-2a} 
    a+c&=\frac{2bd-2a^2}{b+d-2a} 
    b+e&=\frac{b^2d+bd^2-a^2b-a^2d}{d(b+d-2a)} 
\end{align*}

Now we have
\begin{align*}
    \frac{be(a+c)-ac(b+e)}{be-ac}&=\frac{a(2bd-ab-ad)(b(2bd-2a^2)-b^2d-bd^2+a^2b+a^2d)}{d(b+d-2a)^2} \cdot \frac{d(b+d-2a)}{a(b-d)(2bd-ab-ad)} 
    &= \frac{b^2d-a^2b-bd^2+a^2d}{(b+d-2a)(b-d)} 
    &= \frac{bd-a^2}{b+d-2a} 
    &= \frac{a+c}{2}
\end{align*}

