**Redeem**, noticing that  $f(x) = 2x^2 - 7x + 5$  is the whole point of the problem; **Caelestor**'s post describes a method by which this polynomial can be found. 

<details><summary>Fast and rigorous</summary>There is a well-known lemma that states that if  $a$  and  $b$  are positive integers and  $f(x) = \sum_{k=0}^n c_k x^k$  is a polynomial with integer coefficients, then  $\frac{f(a) - f(b)}{a-b}$  is always an integer. The proof is simple: 
\begin{align*}
\frac{f(a) - f(b)}{a-b} 
&= \frac{\sum_{k=0}^n c_k a^k - \sum_{k=0}^n c_k b^n}{a-b} 
&= \frac{\sum_{k=0}^n c_k (a^k - b^k)}{a-b} 
&= \sum_{k=0}^n c_k \left(\frac{a^k - b^k}{a-b}\right) 
&= 0 + \sum_{k=1}^n c_k (a^{k-1} + a^{k-2} b + \cdots + b^{k-1}) \in \mathbb{Z}.
\end{align*}
As  $7-1$  must divide  $f(7) - f(1) = f(7)$ , we see that  $f(7) = 54$ ; as  $8-1$  must divide  $f(8) - f(1) = f(8)$ , we see that  $f(8) = 72$ . The Lagrange interpolation formula (or just solving a system of linear equations) therefore reveals that  $f(x) = 2x^2 - 7x + 5$ . The rest is simple.</details>

