Let  $R$  be the unit square region with vertices  $A=(0, 0), B=(0, 1), C=(1, 1), D=(1, 0)$  and let  $x=(p, q)$  be a random point inside the unit square. In order for the rays and the boundary of the square to only determine triangles and no quadrilaterals, we need to draw four rays from  $x$  to the corners of the square, as shown: [asy]
pair A = (0, 0);
pair B = (0, 1);
pair C = (1, 1);
pair D = (1, 0);
filldraw(A--B--C--D--cycle, lightgray);
pair x = (0.2549, 0.7434);
label(" $x=(p, q)$ ", x, E);
draw(x--A);
draw(x--B);
draw(x--C);
draw(x--D);
[/asy]
The areas of the four regions west, north, east, and south of  $x$  are  $\tfrac{p}{2}, \tfrac{1-q}{2}, \tfrac{1-p}{2},$  and  $\tfrac{q}{2},$  respectively. Select one triangle from each side of square  $ABCD$ : one of the  $a$  triangles from side  $\overline{AB},$  one of the  $b$  triangles from  $\overline{BC},$  one of the  $c$  triangles from  $\overline{CD},$  and one of the  $d$  triangles from side  $\overline{DA}$ . Since all triangles' areas are equal, we can create the following equation: \[\dfrac{p}{2a}=\dfrac{1-q}{2b}=\dfrac{1-p}{2c}=\dfrac{q}{2d}.\] Take  $\tfrac{p}{2a}=\tfrac{1-p}{2c}$  and cross-multiply to get \[2cp=2a-2ap\rightarrow 2a=2p(a+c)\rightarrow p=\tfrac{a}{a+c}.\] Similarly, take  $\tfrac{q}{2d}=\tfrac{1-q}{2b}$  and cross-multiply again to get \[2bq=2d-2dq\rightarrow 2d=2q(b+d)\rightarrow q=\tfrac{d}{b+d}.\] Notice that  $a+c=b+d=50$  since together the regions north and south of  $x$  occupy half of the square region (and thus contain half of the  $100$  triangles), as do the regions east and west of  $x$ . Therefore  $100$ -ray partitional points have the form  $(p, q)=(\tfrac{a}{50}, \tfrac{d}{50})$  for positive integers  $d$  and  $a$ . By a similar logic we can show that  $60$ -ray partitional points have the form  $(p^\prime, q^\prime)=(\tfrac{a^{\prime}}{30}, \tfrac{d^\prime}{30})$  for positive integers  $d^\prime$  and  $a^\prime$ . All  $100$ -ray partitional points of the form  $(p, q)=(\tfrac{a}{50}, \tfrac{d}{50})$  for positive integers  $d$  and  $a$  are such that  $0<\tfrac{d}{50}, \tfrac{a}{50}<1$ . The number of such points is  $49^{2}$ . If such a point also has the second form, then  $\tfrac{d}{50}=\tfrac{d^\prime}{30}$  and  $\tfrac{a}{50}=\tfrac{a^\prime}{30}$ . These equations simplify to  $d=\tfrac{5}{3}d^\prime$  and  $a=\tfrac{5}{3}a^\prime$ . Therefore  $5\mid d, a$ . The number of choices for  $d$  and  $a$  in this case are each  $\lfloor\tfrac{49}{5}\rfloor=9$  and therefore  $9^{2}=81$  such points are also  $60$ -ray partitional. The requested number of points is  $49^{2}-9^{2}=\boxed{\textbf{(C)} ~2320}$ .

