Choose the person who recieves what they ordered in  $9$  ways. WLOG say a beef person got their meal. Then we have,
\begin{align*}
\text{Remaining Meals: } BBCCCFFF
\text{Remaining People: } BBCCCFFF
\end{align*}
Then assume both beef meals go to chicken people (or both go to fish people), which we can choose in  $3$  ways, so we have,
\begin{align*}
\text{Remaining Meals: } CCCFFF
\text{Remaining People: } BBCFFF
\end{align*}
Then we only have one way to give the fish meals, and one way to give the chicken meals so this case  $2 \cdot 3 = 6$  ways.  For the other case assume both beef meals go to one chicken and one fish person. We can choose these people in  $3 \cdot 3 = 9$  ways. Then we have,
\begin{align*}
\text{Remaining Meals: } CCCFFF
\text{Remaining People: } BBCCFF
\end{align*}
Now clearly one beef person takes a chicken and the other beef person takes a fish, and then remaining chicken people take fish meals and remaining fish people take chicken meals. We have  $2$  ways to do this. Thus this case gives  $2 \cdot.9 = 18$  ways.

 Then our final count is  $9 \cdot (6 + 18) = 9 \cdot 24 = 216$ .

