Very nice problem!  I add one more approach.

We call a prime  $p$  **bad** if and only if  $p\mid S_p$  and call it **good** otherwise. Call prime  $p$  **very bad** if and only if  $p^t \mid S_n$  for every  $t\in\mathbb{Z}^+$  and sufficientely large  $n$ . We also define  $f(n)=\nu_p(n!)$  for convenience. We prove the following observation on very bad primes.**Claim 1:**For any very bad prime  $p$  and  $k\in\mathbb{Z}^+$ ,  $p^{f(pk)+1}\mid S_{kp}$ .**Proof :** By definition of very bad prime,  $p^{f((k+1)p}\mid S_{(k+1)p}\implies p^{f(pk)+1}\mid S_{(k+1)p}$ . Note that
\begin{align*}
S_{(k+1)p} &= S_{kp} + (kp)!\left[(kp+1) + (kp+1)(kp+2) +...+(kp+1)(kp+2)...(kp+(p-1)\right] 
&\equiv S_{kp} + (kp)!(1!+2!+...+(p-1)!) \pmod{p^{f(pk)+1}} 
&\equiv S_{kp} \pmod{p^{f(pk)+1}} 
\end{align*}
where we have use the fact that all very bad primes are bad. Hence  $p^{f(pk)+1}\mid S_{kp}$  as desired.

Now for any very bad prime  $p$ , call integer  $n$ ,  $p$ -tasty if and only if  $\nu_p(S_n) >\nu_p(n!)$ . We claim that**Claim 2 :** For any very bad prime  $p$  and  $k,r\in\mathbb{Z}^+$  where  $r<p$ , the number  $kp+r$  is  $p$ -tasty if and only if  $p\mid S_r$ .**Proof :** Observe that  $f(kp+r) = f(kp)$ . Therefore  $kp+r$  is  $p$ -tasty  $\iff p^{f(kp)+1}\mid S_r$  which is equivalent to
\begin{align*}
 &p^{f(kp)+1}\mid (kp+1)!+(kp+2)!+...+(kp+r)! 
&\iff p\mid (kp+1) + (kp+1)(kp+2) +... + (kp+1)(kp+2)...(kp+r) 
&\iff p\mid 1!+2!+...+r!
\end{align*}
implying the claim.

Now suppose that  $S_n$  has no prime divisor greater than  $k$ . This implies that set of all bad primes is finite. Let  $p_1, p_2,...,p_r$  are all very bad primes and let  $q_1,q_2,...,q_s$  be all bad but not very bad prime. Clearly for all  $i=1,2...,s$ , there exists  $t_i$  such that  ${q_i}^{t_i}\nmid S_n$  for every  $n$ .

Take  $n\equiv 1\pmod{p_1p_2...p_r}$  and  $n$  sufficiently large. Clearly by Claim 2,  $n$  is not  $p_i$ -tasty for all  $i$ . Therefore  $\nu_{p_i}(S_n) \le\nu_{p_i}(n!)$  and  $\nu_{q_i}(S_n) < t_i$ . This implies
\begin{align*}
S_n &\le \prod_{p \text{ good}} p^{\nu_p(S_n)} \prod_{i=1}^{r} {p_i}^{\nu_{p_i}(n!)} \prod_{i=1}^{s} {q_i}^{t_i} 
&< A\prod_{p \text{ good}} p^{\nu_p(S_n)}   \prod_{i=1}^{r} {p_i}^{\frac{n}{p_i-1}} 
&< AB^n \prod_{p \text{ good}} p^{\nu_p(S_n)}
\end{align*}
for some constant  $A, B$ . Clearly if  $n$  is large enough, the last product must be greater than one. This implies there exists some good prime dividing  $S_n$ . However, any good prime dividing  $S_n$  must be greater than  $n$  which is contradiction.

