Let  $E'$  be the intersection of the ray  $HM$  with  $\Gamma$ ,  $I$  and  $G$  are feet from  $B$  and  $C$ , respectively. Point  $K$  is defined as the intersection between  $BC$  and  $AE$ .

By relfecting the orthocenter we have that  $90=\angle E'EA =\angle MEA = \angle HEA$ .
Meaning that  $AEDM$  is cyclic, and that  $E \in (AGH)$ . By the theory of the radical center we have that the radical axes of  $(AGHI)$ ,  $(ABC)$  and  $(BIGC)$  is the common point  $K$ .

Meaning that  $-1=(K,D;B,C)=(B,C;K,D)$ . By the midpoint lemma we have that:  $$ KD.DM=DB.DC=ED.DF $$  by power of a point this implies that  $EKFM$  is cyclic.
Now we have that:  $$ \angle DMF = \angle KMF = \angle KEF = \angle DMA $$  meaning that  $AF$  is the  $A$ -symmedian of  $ABC$ , implying that  $\frac{AB}{AC} = \frac{FB}{FC}$ .

We claim that  $F$  lies on the  $A$ -symmedian which satisfies the desired ratios. To show this, let  $F’$  be the intersection of the  $A$ -symmedian and the circumcircle, other than  $A$ . We will show that lines  $MH$  and  $DF’$  meet on the circumcircle which is enough to show  $F’=F$ .

Employ barycentrics on  $\triangle ABC$  with  $A=(1,0,0),B=(0,1,0),C=(0,0,1)$ . It is well-known that  $M=(0:1:1),H=(S_{BC},S_{CA},S_{AB}),D=(0:S_C:S_B)$ .

Since  $F'$  is on the  $A$ -symmedian, it can be parameterized by  $F'=(t:b^2:c^2)$  but it is also on  $\Omega$  so  $\text{Pow}_\Omega (F')=0$ . Of course,  $\Omega$  has equation  $a^2yz+b^2zx+c^2xy=0$ , so plugging in  $F'$  gives  $a^2b^2c^2+2tb^2c^2=0 \Rightarrow t=-\frac{a^2}2$ . So  $$ F'=\left(-\frac{a^2}2,b^2,c^2\right)=(-a^2:2b^2:2c^2). $$  Now we will intersect lines  $MH$  and  $DF’$ .

Line  $MH$  is given by   $$ \begin{vmatrix}x&y&z 0&1&1 S_{BC}&S_{CA}&S_{AB}\end{vmatrix}=0 \iff x(S_{AB}-S_{AC})+y(S_{BC})+z(-S_{BC})=0 $$   while line  $DF’$  is given by   $$ \begin{vmatrix}x&y&z 0&S_{C}&S_{B} -a^2&2b^2&2c^2\end{vmatrix}=0 \iff x(2c^2S_{C}-2b^2S_{B})+y(-a^2S_{B})+z(a^2S_{C})=0.  $$   Their point of intersection,  $E$ , is the solution to the system   $$ \begin{bmatrix}1&1&1 S_{AB}-S_{AC}&S_{BC}&-S_{BC} 2c^2S_C-2b^2S_B& -a^2S_B&a^2S_C\end{bmatrix}\begin{bmatrix}xyz\end{bmatrix}=\begin{bmatrix}100.\end{bmatrix} $$   We will solve this with Cramer’s Rule. Since we only need the unhomogenized coordinates of  $E$ , we will not compute the denominator. Cramer’s Rule tells us that \begin{align*}\bullet x&=\begin{vmatrix}S_{BC}&-S_{BC} -a^2S_B&a^2S_C\end{vmatrix}&= a^2S_{BC}(S_C-S_B)&=-a^2S_{BC}(c^2-b^2)\end{align*}

\begin{align*}\bullet y&=\begin{vmatrix}-S_{BC}&S_{AB}-S_{AC} -a^2S_C&2c^2S_C-2b^2S_b\end{vmatrix}&= S_{BC}(2b^2S_B-2c^2S_C)-a^2S_C(S_{AB}-S_{AC})&= S_C[2S_{B}(2b^2S_B-2c^2S_C)-a^2S_A(S_{B}-S_{C})]&=S_{CA}(c^2-b^2)[2S_B-a^2]&= S_{CA}(c^2-b^2)(c^2-b^2)\end{align*}

\begin{align*}\bullet z&=\begin{vmatrix}-S_{AB}-S_{AC}&S_{BC} 2c^2S_C-2b^2S_b&--a^2S_B\end{vmatrix}&= S_{BC}(2b^2S_B-2c^2S_C)-a^2S_B(S_{AB}-S_{AC})&= S_B[2S_{C}(2b^2S_B-2c^2S_C)-a^2S_A(S_{B}-S_{C})]&=S_{BA}(c^2-b^2)[2S_C-a^2]&= S_{BA}(c^2-b^2)(-c^2+b^2)\end{align*}

Where we simplified using  $b^2S_B-c^2S_C=\frac12(a^2b^2-b^4+b^2c^2-a^2c^2-b^2c^2+c^4)=\frac12[a^2(b^2-c^2)-(b^2+c^2)(b^2-c^2)]=(c^2-b^2)S_A$  and  $S_B-S_C=c^2-b^2$ . Hence, upon factoring  $c^2-b^2$ , we find  $E=(-a^2S_{BC}:(-b^2+c^2)S_{AC}:(b^2-c^2)S_{BA})$ . 

Finally, observe \begin{align*}\text{Pow}_{(ABC)}€&=a^2[(-b^2+c^2)S_{AC}][(b^2-c^2)S_{BA}]+b^2[(b^2-c^2)S_{BA}][-a^2S_{BC}]+c^2[-a^2S_{BC}][(-b^2+c^2)S_{AC}]&= a^2S_{ABC}[S_A(-b^2+c^2)(b^2-c^2)-b^2S_B(b^2-c^2)-c^2S_C(-b^2+c^2)]&= a^2S_{ABC}[-S_A(-b^2+c^2)^2+(b^2S_B-c^2S_C)(-b^2+c^2)]&= a^2S_{ABC}[-S_A(-b^2+c^2)^2+S_A(-b^2+c^2)^2]&=0\end{align*} so  $E$  is on the circumcircle which completes the proof.

