The answer is  $\boxed{5}$ . 

A quick Solution and Motivation: probably the most instructive way a problem can teach you IVT and extremal simultaneously (both of which I think are algebraic techniques, in a combinatorial problem).  $\color{green} \rule{25cm}{2pt}$  $\color{green} \textbf{Value Control.}$  Define an  $\textit{intersection cell}$  of the configuration to be a cell  $I_C$  so that the sum of rows above it, rows under it, columns at the left of it, and columns at the right of it are each at most  $1$ . 

Then, the box exhibits an  $\textit{intersection cell}$ .  $\color{green} \rule{25cm}{0.4pt}$   $\color{green} \textbf{Proof.}$  Consider a row  $r$  where the sum of the first  $r$  rows first exceed  $1$ , and consider a column  $c$  similarly. We will prove that the cell  $I_C$  in row  $r$ , column  $c$  is an intersection cell. 

However, this is incredibly easy (coming up with this Claim is the hard part); for the extremal condition require the top  $r-1$  rows' sum to be  $1$  or less, and the leftmost  $c-1$  columns similarly. 

Moreover, the problem condition require the bottom  $2012-r$  rows to have a sum of at most  $1$ , since the topmost  $r$  rows already exceed  $1$ . The rightmost  $2012-c$  columns are analogous.  $\blacksquare$   $\blacksquare$   $\color{red} \rule{25cm}{2pt}$   $\color{red} \textbf{Finishing Touches.}$  Let the sum of the squares which is strictly at the top-left of  $I_C$  to be  $a$ , directly at the top to be  $b$ , strictly top-right  $c$ , directly left  $d$ , exactly  $I_C$   $e$ , directly right  $f$ , strictly bottom-left  $g$ , directly bottom  $h$ , strictly bottom-right  $i$ . 

Then,  $a+b+c \leq 1$ ,  $a+d+g \leq 1$ ,  $c+f+i \leq 1$ ,  $g+h+i \leq 1$  and  $e \leq 1$ . Summing all that, it becomes
\[ 2a+b+2c+d+e+f+2g+h+2i \leq 1+1+1+1+1 = 5\]
with equality (sum of  $a$  to  $i$  equalling five) iff  $a,c,g,i$  all equals zero;  $b = 1-0-0 = d = f = i$ , and  $e = 1$ . 

Not only does we obtain an upper bound  $(5)$ , but also a configuration which equality happens and  $\textit{all}$  possible configurations which equality happens. We are done.  $\blacksquare$   $\blacksquare$   $\blacksquare$  

<details><summary>Motivation: extending and distributing.</summary>Let  $2012 = n$ .  $\color{cyan} \rule{25cm}{2pt}$   $\color{green} \text{Basic Extensions and Exploration.}$  Basically this started with considering the sum of each individual row and column like this: 
[list = disc][*] either  $r_1$  or  $r_2+\ldots+r_n$  is at most  $1$ , [*] either  $r_1+r_2$  or  $r_3+\ldots+r_n$  is at most  $1$ , [*] and so on, until [*] either  $r_1+\ldots+r_{n-1}$  or  $r_n$  is at most  $1$ . [/list]
The first configuration I directly thought was to ensure one big row/one big column, and that can be done by letting 
\[ r_1+r_2+\ldots+r_{n-1} \leq 1\]
to free whatever  $r_n$  may be. Upon further inspection, doing this with column  $c_n$  granted me the configuration
[asy]usepackage("tikz");label("\begin{tikzpicture}[scale=0.8] 
\foreach \i in {1,2,3} \draw[blue!30!white,very thin] (0,\i)--(4,\i); \foreach \j in {1,2,3} \draw[blue!30!white,very thin] (\j,0)--(\j,4); 
\draw[blue,very thick] (0,0)--(4,0)--(4,4)--(0,4)--cycle; \node at (0.5,3.5) { $1$ }; \node at (3.5,0.5){ $n$ };
\end{tikzpicture}");[/asy]
But an  $n$  in the last cell is strictly prohibited by the bound on each cell. It seemed like the problem were out of reach then, as the existence of each cell's bound guaranteed the total to be bounded (so any attempt to enlarge the cells artificially would fail.) 
Then, seeing the last row's sum  $r_n$  is at most  $n$ , still guessing the answer to the problem to be  $O(n)$ , I tried distributing the last cell's value to each cell of  $r_n$ , 
[asy]usepackage("tikz");label("\begin{tikzpicture}[scale=0.8] 
\foreach \i in {1,2,3} \draw[blue!30!white,very thin] (0,\i)--(4,\i); \foreach \j in {1,2,3} \draw[blue!30!white,very thin] (\j,0)--(\j,4); 
\draw[blue,very thick] (0,0)--(4,0)--(4,4)--(0,4)--cycle; \node at (0.5,3.5) { $1$ }; \node at (3.5,0.5){ $n$ };
\end{tikzpicture}
\begin{tikzpicture}
\node at (0,0) {}; \node at (0,1.5) { $\quad \Rightarrow \quad$ };
\end{tikzpicture}
\begin{tikzpicture}[scale=0.8] 
\fill[red!8!white] (0,0)--(0,4)--(2,4)--(2,0)--cycle; \fill[orange!8!white,shift={(2,0)}] (0,0)--(0,4)--(2,4)--(2,0)--cycle;
\foreach \i in {1,2,3} \draw[blue!30!white,very thin] (0,\i)--(4,\i); \foreach \j in {1,2,3} \draw[blue!30!white,very thin] (\j,0)--(\j,4); 
\draw[blue,very thick] (0,0)--(4,0)--(4,4)--(0,4)--cycle; \node at (0.5,3.5) { $1$ }; \node[green] at (3.5,0.5){ $1$ }; \node[green] at (2.5,0.5){ $1$ }; \node[green] at (1.5,0.5){ $1$ }; \node[green] at (0.5,0.5){ $1$ };
\end{tikzpicture}");[/asy]
but this would create problems for the column's section. 

So I've given up on creating a wild construction and instead looking for patterns to bound the total sum of cells. On  $n = 2$ , it's immediately clear from the condition
\[ \text{either} \ r_1 \leq 1 \ \text{or} \ r_2 \leq 1 \]
and I immediately got  $r_1+r_2 \leq 1 + 2 = 3$  --- and construction is straightforward too: one row must be entirely filled with  $1$  and there can't be a distribution of  $a$  and  $1-a$  in the other row, as it would make the column's sum equal to  $a+1$  and  $2-a$  (which is prohibited unless  $a = 0$  or  $a = 1$ .) 

Upon extending from  $n = 2$  to  $n = 3$ , I could add an empty row and column to the left and to the top of a  $n = 2$  config such as
[asy]usepackage("tikz");label("\begin{tikzpicture}[scale=0.8]
\foreach \i in {0,1,2} \draw[blue,thick] (0,\i)--(2,\i); \foreach \i in {0,1,2} \draw[blue,thick] (\i,0)--(\i,2);
