\begin{tikzpicture}[scale=0.8]
\fill[red!5!white] (0,0)--(4,0)--(4,4)--(3,4)--(3,1)--(0,1)--cycle;
\foreach \i in {0,1,2,3,4} \draw[blue,thick] (0,\i)--(4,\i); \foreach \i in {0,1,2,3,4} \draw[blue,thick] (\i,0)--(\i,4);
\node at (1.5,1.5) { $1$ }; \node at (2.5,1.5) { $1$ }; \node at (2.5,2.5) { $1$ }; 
\end{tikzpicture}");[/asy] $\color{cyan} \rule{25cm}{2pt}$   $\color{green} \text{First Dummy Construction.}$  But this already seemed too spacious for  $n = 4$ . So, as the condition says that whenever
\[ r_1+r_2+\ldots+r_i > 1 \Rightarrow r_{i+1}+\ldots+r_n \leq 1\]
the second condition must be true for  $i = 3$  in this case, i.e. the sum of numbers in the fourth column must be less than  $1$  whenever the first three are overloaded. Keeping this in mind, I moved some values from the third to the fourth as a balancing countermeasure:
[asy]usepackage("tikz");label("\begin{tikzpicture}[scale=0.8]
\foreach \i in {0,1,2,3,4} \draw[blue,thick] (0,\i)--(4,\i); \foreach \i in {0,1,2,3,4} \draw[blue,thick] (\i,0)--(\i,4);
\node at (1.5,1.5) { $1$ }; \node at (2.5,1.5) { $1$ }; \node at (2.5,2.5) { $1$ }; 
