If  $n\in A_{2h+1}$  for some  $h$  then switch the above coloration. Let now  $m$  be a generic integer and color it as its prime factor with highest exponent: if there is ambiguity, pick the largest of these primes. Consider now the sequence  $a, ar, ar^2, \ldots$ , for some  $a,r\in\mathbb{N}$ . From a certain point, the primes of  $r$  will dominate over those of  $a$ , and the color of the generic element  $ar^N$ , of the sequence will be the color of  $p^{r_0N+a_0}$ ,  for some fixed  $r_0,a_0$ . It's not difficult to see that there are infinitely many white and black elements of this form, for any fixed  $r_0,a_0$  and prime  $p$ .

