We use  $\odot X$  to signify a circle centered at  $X.$ The big circle is a circle centered at  $O$  with radius  $6,$  the blue circle is a circle centered at  $A$  with radius  $4,$  and the half-blue, half-black circle,  $\odot X,$  is the completion of the semicircle mentioned in part **(2)** of the problem. (The blue represents the semicircle in the prompt.) Let  $E = \overline{AC} \cap \overline{BD}.$ Note that  $B, D$  are the intersections of  $\odot A$  and  $\odot O$  since  $OB = OD = 6$  and  $AB = AD = 4.$  Since  $ABCD$  is a rhombus,  $E$  perpendicularly bisects both  $\overline{AC}$  and  $\overline{BD}.$  We claim that  $O, A, C$  lie on the same line. Since  $\overleftrightarrow{BD}$  is the radical axis of  $\odot A$  and  $\odot O$  we have  $\overleftrightarrow{BD} \perp \overleftrightarrow{AO}.$  But since  $\overline{AC} \perp \overline{BD}$  as well we have  $O, A, C$  collinear.

Let  $A = (x, y).$  Since  $A$  lies on  $\odot X$  we can substitute  $A = (x, y)$  into the circle equation to get  $A = \left(x, \pm \sqrt{4x - x^2}\right).$  Thus,  $x^2 + y^2 = 4x = OA^2.$  We then use Law of Cosines on  $\triangle{OAB}$  to calculate  $\cos{\angle BAO}.$    $$ \cos{\angle BAO} = \dfrac{OA^2 + AB^2 - OB^2}{2 \cdot OA \cdot AB} = \dfrac{4x + 16 - 36}{2 \cdot 2 \sqrt{x} \cdot 4} = \dfrac{x - 5}{4 \sqrt{x}} \iff \cos{\left(180 - \angle BAO\right) = \cos \angle BAC} = \dfrac{5 - x}{4 \sqrt{x}} $$  So  $AE = \frac{5 - x}{\sqrt{x}} \iff AC = \frac{10 - 2x}{\sqrt{x}} \iff OC = OA + AC = \frac{10}{\sqrt{x}}.$  The product  $OA \cdot OC$  is  $20,$  proving part **(1)**.  $\blacksquare$  

The ratio  $\frac{OC}{OA}$  is  $\frac{5}{x},$  and so   $$ C = \frac{5}{x} \cdot A = \left(5, \pm \dfrac{5 \sqrt{4x - x^2}}{x}\right) = \left(5, \dfrac{5y}{x}\right). $$   But semicircle in the prompt restricts  $\frac{y}{x}$  to be in between  $1$  and  $-1$  inclusive. Thus, C takes the form   $$ \boxed{C = (5, y) | -5 \leq y \leq 5}.~\blacksquare $$   If you're not convinced, refresh the page and look at the diagram's coordinates of  $C.$</details>

