<details><summary>Solution</summary>Let  $T$  be the midpoint of  $CE$  (and thus the center of  $\omega$ ). Also let  $a=ME$ ,  $r=TE$  and  $\theta=\angle ZEP$ . We will first establish a few facts before going to the task at hand.

Consider the homothety at  $D$  that sends  $\omega_1$  to  $\Omega$ . Since the tangent at  $F$  is parallel to the tangent at  $M$ , we must have that  $F$  gets sent to  $M$ . Thus,  $D,F,M$  collinear. Similarly, by the homothety at  $Z$  that sends  $\omega_1$  to  $\omega$ , we learn that  $C,Z,F$  collinear since the tangents at  $F$  and  $C$  are parallel.

We now claim that  $\mathrm{pow}_{\omega_1}(M)=MB^2$ . Note that  $\angle FBM=\frac{1}{2}\widehat{MA}=\frac{1}{2}\widehat{MB}=\angle MDB$ . Thus,  $\triangle FBM\sim\triangle MDB$ . Thus,  $MF\cdot MD=MB^2$ , as desired. Note that
\begin{align*}
MA^2 &= EM^2 + AE^2 
&= EM^2 + EC\cdot EM 
&= EM\cdot(EM+EC) 
&= EM\cdot CM,
\end{align*}
so  $\mathrm{pow}_\omega(M)=MA^2=\mathrm{pow}_{\omega_1}(M)$ . Thus,  $M$  lies on the radical axis of  $\omega$  and  $\omega_1$ , so  $MZ$  is tangent to both circles.

Finally, we note that  $F$  is the orthocenter of  $CMP$  since  $MD$  and  $PE$  are altitudes, so we have that  $FE\cdot PE=CE\cdot ME$ . This can be proven by considering the power of  $E$  w.r.t.  $(CMP)$  and noting that the reflection of  $F$  in  $E$  lies on  $(CMP)$ . Thus, we have that
\begin{align*}
\frac{CM}{PE}\tan\theta &= \frac{a+2r}{\frac{CE\cdot ME}{EF}}\tan\theta 
&= \frac{(a+2r)\cdot EF}{2ra}\tan\theta 
&= \frac{(a+2r)\cdot CE\tan\theta}{2ra}\tan\theta 
&= \frac{(a+2r)\tan^2\theta}{a}.
\end{align*}
Now, since  $MZ$  is tangent to  $\omega$ , we have that  $\cos\angle ZTM = \frac{r}{r+a}$ , so  $\cos(2\theta)=\frac{r}{r+a}$ . Therefore,
\begin{align*}
\tan^2\theta &= \frac{1-\cos^2\theta}{\cos^2\theta} 
&= \frac{2-2\cos^2\theta}{2\cos^2\theta} 
&= \frac{2-(\cos(2\theta)+1)}{1+\cos(2\theta)} 
&= \frac{1-\frac{r}{r+a}}{1+\frac{r}{r+a}} 
&= \frac{a}{a+2r}.
\end{align*}
Thus,  $\frac{CM}{PE}\tan\theta=\frac{(a+2r)\tan^2\theta}{a}=1$ , as desired.</details>

