Upon inspection,  $\int_{1}^{\infty} \frac{x^{\alpha}\ln(x)}{(1+x)^{\beta}} dx$ , we necessitate  $\beta-\alpha > 1$  for convergence.  Let us utilize the Comparison Test for this improper integral according to: $\int_{1}^{\infty}\frac{x^{\alpha}\ln(x)}{(1+x)^{\beta}} dx \le \int_{1}^{\infty} \frac{x^{\alpha}x^{p}}{(1+x)^{\beta}} dx$  

of which the RHS integral converges  $\Leftrightarrow \beta -(\alpha + p) > 1$ .  Suppose that  $\ln(x) \le x^{p}$  for all  $x\ge 1$ .  If we solve for  $p$  in terms of  $x$ , we obtain the function  $p=f(x)=\frac{\ln[\ln(x)]}{\ln(x)}$  where  $f'(x)=0$  yields the critical point: $f'(x) = \frac{1 - \ln[\ln(x)]}{x \ln^{2}(x)} = 0 \Rightarrow x = e^{e}$ ;

and  $f''(x)$  at this critical point produces: $f''(x) = \frac{-3 + \ln(x)[-1 + \ln[\ln(x)]] + 2\ln[\ln(x)]}{x^2 \ln^{3}(x)} \Rightarrow f''(e^{e}) = -\frac{1}{e^{2e+3}} < 0$  (a maximum).

This gives us  $p=f(e^{e}) = \frac{1}{e}$ , which means that  $x^{1/e}$  is the supremum of  $\ln(x)$  such that  $\ln(x) \le x^{p}$  for all  $x \in [1, \infty)$ .  Returning to the RHS integral of the original inequality above, we require: $\beta - (\alpha+p) > 1 \Rightarrow \beta > \alpha + 1+\frac{1}{e}$ ;

which gives us the ranges: $\textcolor{red}{\alpha \in \mathbb{R}, \beta \in (\alpha + 1+1/e, \infty)}$ .

