<details><summary>Solution</summary>Let  $a_i = \frac{b_i}{b_{i + 1}}$  for all integers  $2 \leq i \leq n$ , where  $b_{n + 1} = b_2$  and the  $b_i$  are positive reals. 

The inequality is therefore equivalent to 
	\begin{align*}
		\prod_{i = 2}^n (b_i + b_{i + 1})^i &> n^n \cdot b_2^n\prod_{i = 3}^{n} b_i^{i - 1}
	\end{align*}
By weighted AM-GM, we have
	\begin{align*}
		(b_i + b_{i + 1})^i &= \left(\frac{1}{i}\left(ib_i\right) + \frac{i - 1}{i}\left(\frac{i}{i - 1}b_{i + 1}\right)\right)^i 
		&\geq \left((ib_i)^{\frac{1}{i}}\left(\frac{i}{i - 1}b_{i + 1}\right)^{\frac{i - 1}{i}}\right)^i 
		&\geq (ib_i)\left(\frac{i}{i - 1}b_{i + 1}\right)^{i - 1}.
	\end{align*}
Therefore, 
	\begin{align*}
		\prod_{i = 2}^n (b_i + b_{i + 1})^i &\geq \prod_{i = 2}^n i\left(\frac{i}{i - 1}\right)^{i - 1}b_ib_{i + 1}^{i - 1} 
		&\geq \prod_{i = 2}^n i \cdot \prod_{i = 2}^n \left(\frac{i}{i - 1}\right)^{i - 1} \cdot b_2^n\prod_{i = 3}^n b_i^{i - 1} 
		&\geq n! \cdot \frac{n^{n - 1}}{(n - 1)!} \cdot b_2^n\prod_{i = 3}^n b_i^{i - 1} 
		&\geq n^n \cdot b_2^n\prod_{i = 3}^n b_i^{i - 1}.
	\end{align*}

To finish, we need only show that equality is not achievable. We have equality iff we have equality in all the multiplied inequalities above. By the equality case of weighted AM-GM, this is equivalent to
	\begin{align*}
		b_2 &= 2b_3 
		b_3 &= 3b_4 
		&\vdots 
		b_n &= nb_2.
	\end{align*}
Hence, we must have
	\begin{align*}
		b_2 &= b_2n!,
	\end{align*}
so we must have  $b_2 = 0$ , contradicting the fact that the  $b_i$  are all positive reals. This completes the proof.</details>

By AM-GM,

\begin{align*}
(1 + a_k)^k & = \left(\frac{1}{k-1} + \frac{1}{k-1} + \cdots + \frac{1}{k-1} + a_k\right)^k
& \ge \left(k\sqrt[k]{\left(\frac{1}{k-1}\right)^{k-1} \cdot a_k}\right)^k
& = a_k\left(\frac{k^k}{(k-1)^{k-1}}\right)
\end{align*}

Multiplying this for  $k = 2...n$ , we see that

\begin{align*}
\prod (1 + a_k)^k & \ge \left(\frac{2^2}{1^2}\cdot \frac{3^3}{2^2} \cdot \cdots \cdot \frac{n^n}{(n-1)^{n-1}}\right) \cdot a_2\cdot a_3\cdot a_4\cdots a_n
& = n^n.
\end{align*}

Moreover, we see that equality never occurs, since we cannot have all the  $a_k$  equal to  $\tfrac{1}{k-1}$ . This completes the problem.  $\square$

