By AM-GM,
\begin{align*}
\left(\frac1{k-1}+\frac1{k-1}+\cdots+\frac1{k-1}+a_k\right)^k=\left(1+a_k\right)^k &\ge \frac{k^k}{(k-1)^{k-1}} \cdot a_k,
\end{align*}
and so multiplying cyclically we get
\begin{align*}
\prod_{k=1}^n \left(1+a_k\right)^k &\ge \frac{k^k}{(k-1)^{k-1}} \cdot a_k
&=1
\end{align*}
Equality holds when  $a_k=\tfrac1{k-1}$  for all  $1 \le k \le n$ . However, this clearly violates the condition that  $a_2a_3\cdots a_n=1$ , so we're done.

Take (natural) logarithms of both sides to yield the equivalent inequality \[2 \log(1 + a_2) + 3 \log(1 + a_3) + \dots + n \log(1 + a_n) > n \log n\text{.}\] However, for all  $2 \leq i \leq n$ , we have by Jensen
\begin{align*}
    i \log(1 + a_i) &= i \log\left(\frac{1 \cdot i a_i + (i - 1) \cdot \frac{i}{i - 1}}{i}\right) 
    &\geq \log(i a_i) + (i - 1)\log\left(\frac{i}{i - 1}\right) 
    &= \log(i) + \log(a_i) + (i - 1) \log(i) - (i - 1) \log(i - 1) 
    &= \log (a_i) + i \log(i) - (i - 1) \log(i - 1)\text{.}
\end{align*}

Summing over  $i \in \{2, 3, \dots, n\}$  yields
\begin{align*}
    2 \log(1 + a_2) + 3 \log(1 + a_3) + \dots + n \log(1 + a_n) &\geq \log(a_2) + \log(a_3) + \dots + \log(a_n) + n \log(n) 
    &= \log(a_2 a_3 \cdots a_n) + n \log n 
    &= n \log n\text{.}
\end{align*}

Equality holds if and only if for all  $i$  we have  $ia_i = \frac{i}{i - 1}$  or equivalently  $a_i = \frac{1}{i - 1}$ . But then \[a_2 a_3 \cdots a_n = \frac{1}{(n - 1)!} \neq 1\] so the inequality is strict.

