<details><summary>Solution</summary>Let  $\omega_A, \omega_A$  be circles centered at  $A$  and  $B$  with radius  $AC$  and  $BC$ , respectively.
Let  $L' = BX\cap \omega_A$ .**Lemma:**  $\exists \odot(KLK'L')$ Note that  $CD \perp AB \implies$   $CD$  is the radical axis of  $\omega_A$  and  $\omega_B$ \[X\in CD\implies \text{Pow}_{\omega_A}(X) = \text{Pow}_{\omega_B}(X) \implies XK\cdot XK' = XL\cdot XL'\]
Thus,  $K, K', L, L'$  are concyclic.**Main Proof:** $AC\perp BC \implies AC$  is tangential to  $\omega_B$ .
\[\implies AK\cdot AK' = AC^2 = AL^2 \implies AL^2\]
Thus,  $AL$  is tangential to  $(KLK'L')$ .
Similarly,  $BK$  is tangential to  $(KLK'L')$ .

Therefore,  $MK$  and  $ML$  are tangents from  $M$  to  $(KLK'L')$ . Thus,  $MK = ML$ .

