Now we prove that this is optimal. Replace  $999$  by  $3n$ . Construct a bipartite graph  $G = (R, C)$  with  $R_iC_j$  ( $1 \leq i,j \leq 3n$ ) joined iff the cell at  $i$ -th row and  $j$ -th column is colored red. Let  $Q_n$  denote the quantity defined in the question. Now   $$  Q_n = \sum_{(u,v) \not \in G, u \in R, v \in C} \text{deg}[u] \text{deg}[v]  $$     $$  \leq \sum_{(u,v) \not \in G, u \in R, v \in C}\frac{ (\text{deg}[u])^2 + (\text{deg}[v])^2 }{2} \text{ 
         (AM-GM)}  $$     $$  = \sum_{u \in G}\frac{ (\text{deg}[u])^2(3n - \text{deg}[u])}{2}  \text{            (Easy double counting)} $$   Now note  that by taking derivatives,  $f(x) = x^2(3n-x)$  has maximum value  $4n^2$ , so   $$ Q_n \leq \sum_{u \in G} \frac{(\text{deg}[u])^2(3n - \text{deg}[u])}{2}  \leq \sum_{u \in G} 2n^3 = |V| \cdot 2n^3 = 12n^4  $$  , as desired. For this, the equality case is same - this is achieved (for example) when you color the cell in  $i$ -th row and  $j$ -th column red iff  $\lfloor \frac{i}{n} \rfloor = \lfloor \frac{j}{n} \rfloor$ .

