Define  $I_3$  analogously, and let  $I$  denote the incenter of  $\triangle ABC$ .**<span style="color:#f00">Claim:</span>** $ A, B, I_1, I_2$  are concyclic.

*Proof.* Since  $I = \overline{AI_1} \cap \overline{BI_2} \cap \overline{CI_3}$ , it suffices to show that  $IA \cdot II_1 = IB \cdot II_2$ . From the similarities  $\triangle FAE \sim \triangle CAB \sim \triangle FDB$ , we deduce that \[IA \cdot II_1 = AI \cdot AI(1 - AF/AC) = AI^2(1 - \cos A)\] and similarly  $IB \cdot II_2 = BI^2(1 - \cos B)$ . We can compute
\begin{align*}
  \frac{AI^2}{BI^2} &= \frac{r^2 + (s - a)^2}{r^2 + (s - b)^2} = \frac{(s - a)(s - b)(s - c) + s(s - a)^2}{(s - a)(s - b)(s - c) + s(s - b)^2} 
  &= \frac{(s - a)((s - b)(s - c) + s(s - a))}{(s - b)((s - c)(s - a) + s(s - b))} = \frac{bc(s - a)}{ca(s - b)} = \frac{b(b + c - a)}{a(a + c - b)}
\end{align*}
and
\begin{align*}
  \frac{1 - \cos B}{1 - \cos A} &= \frac{1 - (c^2 + a^2 - b^2)/2ca}{1 - (b^2 + c^2 - a^2)/2bc} = \frac{b(b^2 - c^2 - a^2 + 2ca)}{a(a^2 - b^2 - c^2 + 2bc)} = \frac{b(b + c - a)(b - c + a)}{a(a + b - c)(a - b + c)} 
  &= \frac{b(b + c - a)}{a(a - b + c)},
\end{align*}
so upon rearrangement the claim is proved.  $\square$ In the same manner we can prove that  $AI_1I_3C$  and  $BI_2I_3C$  are cyclic, so  $\overline{O_1O_2} \perp \overline{CI_3I}$ . But  $\overline{I_1I_2}$  is parallel to the tangent to  $(AIB)$  at  $I$ , which is perpendicular to  $\overline{CI}$ . Thus  $\overline{I_1I_2} \parallel \overline{O_1O_2}$  as desired.  $\square$

