WLOG  $AC > AB$ . Then,  $\angle XAD=\angle CAD-\angle CAO=\frac{A}{2}-90+B$ , whereas  $\angle ADX=\angle ADC-90=90-\frac{A}{2}-C=180-\frac{A}{2}-C-90=\frac{A}{2}+B-90=\angle XAD$ . So,  $AXD$  is isosceles. Also,  $\angle EDY=\angle BDA=180-B-\frac{A}{2}=\frac{A}{2}+C$ .

Now, note that  $Y'$ , the reflection of  $Y$  over the perpendicular bisector of  $BC$ , lies on circle  $(BCY)$ , and on line  $XD$ . So, we only need to show that  $Y'BXC$  is cyclic. By Power of a Point, this reduces to  $XD\cdot DY'=BD\cdot DC$ .
\begin{align*}
BD\cdot DC&=XD\cdot DY'
\frac{ac}{b+c}\frac{ab}{b+c}&=\frac{AD}{2\cos(\angle ADX)}\cdot DE\tan\angle EDY
\frac{a^2bc}{(b+c)^2}&=\frac{AD}{2\sin\left(\frac{A}{2}+C\right)}\cdot DE\tan\left(\frac{A}{2}+C\right)
&=\frac{AD\cdot DE}{2\cos\left(\frac{A}{2}+C\right)}
&=\frac{AD}{2\cos\left(\frac{A}{2}+C\right)}\cdot(BC-2BD)
&=\frac{AD}{2\cos\left(\frac{A}{2}+C\right)}\cdot\left(a-\frac{2ac}{b+c}\right)
&=\frac{AD}{2\cos\left(\frac{A}{2}+C\right)}\cdot\frac{a(b-c)}{b+c}
\frac{abc}{b+c}&=\frac{AD(b-c)}{2\cos\left(\frac{A}{2}+C\right)}
\frac{4a^2b^2c^2}{(b^2-c^2)^2}&=\frac{AD^2}{\cos^2\left(\frac{A}{2}+C\right)}
\end{align*}
By Stewarts, we get that  $(AD^2+BD\cdot DC)BC=BD\cdot AC^2+CD\cdot AB^2$ , so  $AD^2=\frac{BD\cdot b^2+CD\cdot c^2}{a}-BD\cdot DC=\frac{b^2c+bc^2}{b+c}-\frac{a^2bc}{(b+c)^2}=bc\left(1-\frac{a^2}{(b+c)^2}\right)$ Going back to our equation,
\begin{align*}
\frac{4a^2b^2c^2}{(b^2-c^2)^2}&=\frac{AD^2}{\cos^2\left(\frac{A}{2}+C\right)}
&=\frac{bc\left(1-\frac{a^2}{(b+c)^2}\right)}{\cos^2\left(\frac{A}{2}+C\right)}
\frac{4a^2bc}{(b-c)^2}&=\frac{(b+c)^2-a^2}{\cos^2\left(\frac{A}{2}+C\right)}
\frac{4a^2bc}{(b-c)^2}\cdot\cos^2\left(\frac{A}{2}+C\right)&=b^2+c^2-a^2+2bc
&=2bc(\cos A+1)
\frac{2a^2}{(b-c)^2}\cos^2\left(\frac{A}{2}+C\right)&=\cos A+1
\frac{2a^2}{(b-c)^2}\frac{1+\cos(A+2C)}{2}&=\cos A+1
a^2+a^2\cos(A+2C)&=(b-c)^2\cos A+(b-c)^2
a^2(\cos A\cos 2C-\sin A\sin 2C)&=(b^2+c^2)\cos A-2bc\cos A+(b-c)^2-a^2
a^2\left(\cos A-2\cos A\sin^2 C-2\sin A\sin C\cos C\right)&=(b^2+c^2)\cos A-2bc\cos A+2bc\cos A-2bc
-2a^2\sin C(\cos A\sin C+\sin A\cos C)&=(b^2+c^2)\cos A-a^2\cos A-2bc
-2a^2\sin C\sin(A+C)&=(b^2+c^2-a^2)\cos A-2bc
-2a^2\sin C\sin B&=2bc\left(\cos^2 A-1\right)
a^2\sin C\sin B&=bc\sin^2 A
\frac{\sin C}{c}\cdot\frac{\sin B}{b}&=\left(\frac{\sin A}{a}\right)^2
\end{align*}
which is true by Law of Sines.  $\square$

