Let  $N$  be the midpoint of the minor arc  $\widehat{BC}$ . Also let  $Y'$  denote the reflection of  $Y$  over  $OM$ . Also by  $R$ , we refer to the circumradius.

Firstly, note that the perpendicularity condition gives us that  $XD\parallel OM\parallel EY$ . Moreover as  $M$  is the midpoint of  $DE$ , this gives us that  $N$  is the midpoint of  $DY$ . Now we get that  $DEYY'$  forms a rectangle. Thus  $YY'$  shares the same perpendicular bisector as that of  $DE$  which is equivalent to  $OM$  which is further the perpendicular bisector of  $BC$ . So  $BCYY'$  is an isosceles trapezium and we get that  $BCYY'$  is cyclic.

Now moreover as  $XD\perp BC$  and  $DY'\perp BC$ , we get that  $\overline{X-D-Y'}$  are collinear. Thus to finish, it is enough to show that  $BXCY'$  is cyclic.

We now length bash YAY! :rotfl: Firstly, from PoP we get that  $NM\cdot 2R=NB^2\implies NM=\dfrac{NB^2}{2R}$ . Also from PoP, we get  $NB^2=ND\cdot NA$ . Finally from  $\triangle AXD\sim\triangle AON$ , we get that  $\dfrac{DX}{R}=\dfrac{AD}{AN}$ . Now,
\begin{align*}
    DX\cdot EY&=DX\cdot 2MN
    &=DX\cdot\dfrac{NB^2}{R}
    &=\dfrac{DX}{R}\cdot NB^2
    &=\dfrac{AD}{AN}\cdot NB^2
    &=\dfrac{AD}{AN}\cdot ND\cdot NA
    &=AD\cdot ND
    &=DB\cdot DC
.\end{align*}

This finishes! :stretcher:

Really nice problem! 

Let's begin by extending  $BI$  to meet  the circumcircle of  $\triangle{BFD}$  at a point  $M_B$ , which happens to be the midpoint of arc  $FD$  not containing  $B$ . Similarly, let's extend  $CI$  to meet the circumcircle of  $\triangle{CED}$  at a point  $M_C$  which is the midpoint of arc  $ED$  not containing  $C$ . By Miquel's Theorem on  $\triangle{IBC}$ , we have that  $IM_BM_CP$  is cyclic (also can be proven by simple angle chasing). Now, using Ptolemy's Theorem on cyclic quadrilateral  $M_BBFD$ , we have  $FD*BM_B = BF*DM_B + FM_B*BD$ , and since  $FM_B = M_BD$ , we get that  $FD*BM_B = CA*FM_B$ . Denote  $M$  to be the midpoint of  $FD$ , and we know that  $M_BM \perp FD$ , and also let the  $A$ -intouch point be  $H$ , the  $B$ -intouch point be  $S$ , and the  $C$ -intouch point be  $R$ .**<u>Claim</u>**:  $\triangle{M_BMF} \sim \triangle{IBH}$ **<u>Proof</u>**: Standard angle chasing gives us  $\angle{M_BFM} = \angle{M_BFD} = \angle{DBM_B} = \angle{HBI}$ , and so by AA similarity,  $\triangle{M_BMF} \sim \triangle{IBH}$ .      $\square$ From the equation we got from Ptolemy's Theorem and using our claim from above, we have  $BM_B = CA*\frac{FM_B}{FD} = CA*\frac{FM_B}{2FM} = CA*\frac{IB}{2BH}$ , so surprisingly,  $M_B$  and  $M_C$  don't depend on  $D$ , and  $I$  also obviously doesn't depend on  $D$ , and so  $(IM_BM_C)$  doesn't depend on  $D$  as well. Consider when  $D$  is the  $A$ -extouch point (as others have mentioned/used). Let the midpoints of sides  $BC, CA, AB$  be  $M_1, M_2, M_3$  (the extouch points are just the reflections of the intouch points over the midpoints of the sides). Now, we can easily get that  $EC = AS$ . We will now proceed by using Phantom Points, by letting  $X$  be the reflection of  $I$  over  $O$ , and so  $IX = XO$ , so it suffices to show that  $X = P$ . We can see that  $XD \perp BC$ , and similarly,  $XE \perp AC$  and  $XF \perp AB$ . Thus, we get that  $FBDX$  and  $ECDX$  are cyclic, and so that would mean  $X$  is the intersection of the circumcircles of  $\triangle{BFD}$  and  $\triangle{CED}$ , which is the definition of  $P$ , so we get  $X = P$ . To cover the part where  $PI$  can just be any chord of the circumcircle of  $IM_BM_CP$  (which it is not in this case), we have that  $\angle{PFB} = \angle{PM_BI}
 = 90$  because  $PFM_BB$  is cyclic, so that would make  $PI$  the diameter of the circumcircle of  $IM_BM_CP$ , so we are done.  $\square$

