We sort the top row in ascending order, and denote them  $a_1<a_2<a_3<\cdots < a_n$ . Assume  $A$  satisfies
\[\sum_{i=1}^A a_i > \frac{n+1}{4}\]
This implies  $A\cdot a_A > \frac{n+1}{4}$  as well, and by extension  $b_A < 1 - \frac{n+1}{4A}$ . Then, we note that
\begin{align*}
    \sum_{i=A}^n b_i &< (n-A+1) b_A
    &<((n+1)-A) \left(1- \frac{n+1}{4A}\right)
    % &= \frac{1}{4A} ((n+1)-A)(4A - (n+1))
    &= (n+1) - A - \frac{(n+1)^2}{4A} + \frac{n+1}{4}
    &= -\frac{((n+1) + 2A)^2}{4A} + \frac{n+1}{4}
    &< \frac{n+1}{4}
\end{align*}
Thus, if we select the largest  $X$  such that  $\sum_{i=1}^X a_i< \frac{n+1}{4}$ , then  $\sum_{j=1}^{X+1} b_i< \frac{n+1}{4}$ , so we have select the  $a$  in the first  $X$  columns, and  $b$  in the last  $n-X$  columns, and the sum in each row will always be  $< \frac{n+1}{4}$ .
